title: Grudin's account of why group tools fail says skg's design avoids the commonest cause, and that this team's use of skg is evidence of the wrong kind for a stranger.
pid: b01304c6-e5d6-4e4b-89c5-8900caccc1a9
body: |2-
  Read whole in Ed's second dream, 2026-11-15: Jonathan Grudin,
  "Groupware and social dynamics: eight challenges for developers",
  Communications of the ACM 37(1), 1994, at
  https://webpages.charlotte.edu/richter/classes/2006/6010/readings/cacm94.html
  From the failed group tools of the 1980s he names eight causes. The
  first is that most group tools make some people do work whose benefit
  goes to others; electronic mail succeeded where the rest failed
  because its sender, who types, is usually the one who wants the
  message read. Read beside Tay's list of what Jeff says skg is for,
  four of the eight bear on whether the case for skg reaches a person
  who is not Jeff, and one on this team.
